'''amain'''<sup>1</sup> (a-mān′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''main''<sup>1</sup>, force: see ''main''<sup>1</sup>.] With force, strength, or violence; violently; furiously; suddenly; at full speed; hastily.
{{smaller block|[He] comes on ''amain'', speed in his look.
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1304.}}
The soul strives ''amain'' to live and work through all things.
{{right|''Emerson'', Compensation.}}
Smote ''amain'' the hollow oak-tree.
{{right|''Longfellow'', Hiawatha, xvii.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To let go''' or '''strike amain''' (''naut.''), to let fall or lower quickly or suddenly: but see ''amain''<sup>2</sup>.}}

'''amain'''<sup>2</sup>† (a̤-mān′), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''amayne'', ''ameyne'', < OF. ''amener'', mod. F. ''amener'', bring to, conduct, induce; naut., haul: ''amener les voiles'', strike sail, ''amener pavillon'', or simply ''amener'', strike flag, surrender; < ''a-'' (< L. ''ad'', to) + ''mener'', lead, conduct, < LL. ''minare'', drive, L. deponent ''minari'', threaten, menace: see ''menace''. Cf. ''amenable''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To lead; conduct; manage.
{{smaller block|That his majesty may have the ''ameyning'' of the matters.
{{right|Quoted in ''Strype'', Eccl. Mem., II. 418. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}
'''2.''' To lower (a sail), especially the topsail.
{{smaller block|He called to us to ''amaine'' our sailes, which we could not well doe.
{{right|''R. Hawkins'', Voyage to South Sea.}}
When you let anything downe into the Howle, lowering it by degrees, they say, ''Amaine''; and being downe, Strike. . . . When you would lower a yard so fast as you can, they call ''Amaine''.
{{right|''Smith'', Seaman’s Gram., vii. 33, ix. 40. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

